<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gpx version="1.1" creator="brooksgroves.com" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1">
<metadata><name>Washington pika sites worth a look (pre-1950, nothing recorded nearby since 2000)</name><link href="https://brooksgroves.com/blog/pikas-part-2-washington.html"><text>Pikas, Part 2: Washington's Quiet Rockpiles</text></link></metadata>
<wpt lat="46.98140" lon="-121.09040"><ele>816</ele><name>Milk Creek, near the Naches highway</name><desc>Label: "NACHREES HWY, NEAR; MILK CREEK" (1940, 2 specimens). Nearest pika record since 2000: 7.1 km. Getting there: SR 410 corridor near Milk Creek. Rock slides by the highway. Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.59970" lon="-119.74450"><ele>1219</ele><name>Funk Mountain, north of Conconully</name><desc>Label: "16MI N CONCONULLY,FUNK MT,4000FT" (1940, 1 specimen). Nearest pika record since 2000: 14.0 km. Getting there: Funk Mountain lookout, north of Conconully (the label says 16 mi N, so it may mean higher country beyond). Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.08690" lon="-121.37920"><ele>1503</ele><name>Naches Pass</name><desc>Label: "NACHES PASS" (1940, 1 specimen). Nearest pika record since 2000: 5.9 km. Getting there: The old wagon route over the crest. Rough forest roads. Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.31470" lon="-121.76110"><ele>1523</ele><name>Mount Higgins lookout</name><desc>Label: "Mount Higgins; Lookout Station" (1940, 2 specimens). Nearest pika record since 2000: 13.6 km. Getting there: Mount Higgins trail off SR 530. Steep. Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.87550" lon="-121.36370"><ele>1585</ele><name>Whatcom Pass</name><desc>Label: "Whatcom Pass" (1920, 3 specimens). Nearest pika record since 2000: 7.9 km. Getting there: Whatcom Pass via Hannegan Pass and the Chilliwack. Backpack. Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.60750" lon="-118.47840"><ele>1682</ele><name>Sherman Pass, Kettle River Range</name><desc>Label: "Sherman Creek Pass; Sherman Pass" (1938, 1947, 7 specimens). Nearest pika record since 2000: 81.7 km. Getting there: Kettle Crest Trail from Sherman Pass, SR 20. Easy access; listen for the eep. Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="47.24330" lon="-120.55920"><ele>1894</ele><name>Table Mountain, Wenatchee National Forest</name><desc>Label: "Table Mountain, Wenatchee National Forest" (1949, 2 specimens). Nearest pika record since 2000: 4.8 km. Getting there: Forest roads north of Ellensburg, around Haney Meadow. Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
<wpt lat="48.93000" lon="-119.97000"><ele>2395</ele><name>Windy Peak, Pasayten</name><desc>Label: "Windy Peak" (1947, 1 specimen). Nearest pika record since 2000: 3.9 km. Getting there: Pasayten Wilderness. A long trail day. Listen for the eep; report sightings to iNaturalist or WDFW's Pika Observation survey.</desc></wpt>
</gpx>